---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Cascadia Systems">
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero-topo section bg-gradient-to-b from-dark-bg via-dark-surface to-dark-bg">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h1 class="mb-8 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
          Systems that replace the work your staff does by hand.
        </h1>
        <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            Cascadia Systems is a data-systems practice in Portland, Oregon. It builds the
            connections between the places an organization's data lives, a CRM, a legacy
            database, a document store, a payment processor, the spreadsheets that hold things
            together, and turns the recurring manual work between them into jobs that run on a
            schedule and log what they did.
          </p>
          <p>
            The main engagement is thirty days long and costs $12,000. It maps where the hours go,
            automates the process costing the most of them, and leaves a plan for the rest.
            Details on the <a href="/services/" class="link">services page</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-10 flex flex-col gap-4 sm:flex-row">
          <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary">Get in touch</a>
          <a href="/services/" class="btn btn-secondary">The thirty-day engagement</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Examples -->
  <section class="section border-t border-dark-border">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-8 text-3xl font-bold">The kind of work this is</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            A membership organization was receiving scanned paper applications and paying staff
            to retype them. An extractor now reads each scan, pulls the fields into a structured
            record, checks them against the rules the staff had been applying in their heads, and
            routes anything uncertain to a person rather than guessing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same organization's payment processor stopped forwarding card transactions and
            nobody upstream noticed for six weeks. The gap surfaced when bank deposits were
            matched against the membership system line by line, a tedious thing to do once and
            a trivial thing to do every night. It runs every night now.
          </p>
          <p>
            A financial analysis that an analyst built by hand, one workbook per district for
            every district in the state, now fills itself in from the state's published data.
            The analyst still does the analysis. He no longer does the typing.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of these took more than a few weeks. In each case the hard part was not the
            code but the time spent with the person doing the job, learning what they were
            actually checking for, which is never quite what the procedure document says.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Limits -->
  <section class="section bg-dark-surface">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-8 text-3xl font-bold">What this is not</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            It is one engineer, Devin MacArthur. That means one or two engagements at a time,
            no subcontractors, and straight answers about scheduling. An organization that needs a platform rebuilt by a
            team of six is the wrong fit, and will be told so on the first call, along with a
            suggestion of who to ask instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also not an AI shop in the sense the phrase has come to mean. A fair share of
            the processes examined need a scheduled script and a shared table, not a model. When
            that is the answer, that is the answer, and the thirty days cost the same.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Data -->
  <section class="section border-t border-dark-border">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-8 text-3xl font-bold">Where the data goes</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            Nowhere, where possible. Work starts with read-only access, and write access is
            requested only for the one thing being automated. Sensitive processing runs on the
            client's machines or on hardware Cascadia Systems owns in Portland, with no outside
            model provider involved. When a hosted model is the right tool, the provider is named
            in the scope along with its data-processing agreement. At the end of an engagement,
            every credential is rotated back to the client and working copies are deleted, in
            writing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="section bg-dark-surface">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
        <h2 class="mb-6 text-3xl font-bold">If this sounds like your problem</h2>
        <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-text-secondary">
          Send a paragraph about the process that costs the most time. Thirty minutes on a call
          is enough to know whether it's a fit.
        </p>
        <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary">devin.macarthur@cascadiasystems.org</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
